  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q21-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "21",
    "prompt": "Let $(n-1)x^2+nx+(n+1)=0$ be a quadratic equation with coefficients that are consecutive integers. For what values of $n\\in\\mathbb Z$ will this equation have two solutions?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=0$ or $n=-1$.\n\nRequire $\\Delta>0$ and exclude $n=1$, which makes the equation linear.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=n^2-4(n-1)(n+1)\\\\\\Delta&=n^2-4(n^2-1)\\\\\\Delta&=4-3n^2.\\end{align*}$$\nTwo distinct real solutions require $4-3n^2>0$, so $n^2<\\frac43$. Since $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, the candidates are $-1,0,1$. When $n=1$, the coefficient of $x^2$ is zero, so the equation is not quadratic. Therefore $n=-1$ or $n=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 54
  },
